To create the desired LaTeX code for drawing the chessboard, we need to use the TikZ package. We will define a grid representing the chessboard and then fill in the squares with the appropriate colors and patterns. The chessboard is an 8x8 grid with alternating light and dark squares. 

The LaTeX code will start with the document class and necessary packages. We will then define a TikZ picture environment where we can draw the chessboard. Inside this environment, we will create a grid of nodes representing the squares of the chessboard. We will use a loop to iterate over the rows and columns, drawing each square and filling it with the appropriate color. 

To represent the state of the chessboard, we need to place the pieces on the appropriate squares. We can use LaTeX commands or symbols to represent the different types of chess pieces.

Here is a simple representation:
```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tikz}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
    \foreach \i in {1,...,8} 
        \foreach \j in {1,...,8} {
            \pgfmathparse{mod(\i+\j,2) ? "lightgray" : "darkgray"}
            \fill[\pgfmathresult] (\i,\j) rectangle ++(1,1);
            % Add chess pieces here according to the FEN notation
        }
    \draw[step=1,black,thin] (1,1) grid (9,9);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

